Hecks.bluebook "HeartbeatScheduler", version: "2026.05.14.1" do
  vision "Rhythmic command dispatcher. The heartbeat already pulses (`~/miette-state/information/.statusline_heart_phase` is incremented on every statusline render) ; this aggregate turns that pulse into a bluebook-first command dispatcher. Rules register a schedule binding a target command to a cadence (every Nth tick) ; on each Tick the scheduler checks all registered schedules and fires Dispatch for the ones whose cadence matches. i616's first consumer is the macrophage family (autonomic sweeps as sidequests) ; i617's ChaosMonkey is the second. Any 'fire X every N ticks' intent in the framework lands here. Stub shape, 2026-05-14 — the runtime side iterates from this contract."
  category "framework"

  # ============================================================
  # HEARTBEAT SCHEDULER — autonomic dispatch on a cadence
  # ============================================================
  #
  # First draft, 2026-05-14, card i616. The bluebook IS the contract
  # for autonomic dispatch ; the runtime wires the statusline pulse
  # into Tick and the sidequest substrate into Dispatch.
  #
  # ── Two aggregates, dense by design ──
  #
  #   * HeartbeatScheduler — the singleton substrate. Holds the
  #     current tick counter and the registry of Schedule entities
  #     (rule → cadence → target command). Conventional
  #     `identified_by :name` shape for now ; once i611 phase 1
  #     lands singleton: true, this collapses to a true singleton.
  #
  #   * Schedule is a CHILD ENTITY inside HeartbeatScheduler, not
  #     its own aggregate. Schedules live and die with the scheduler ;
  #     they have no identity outside it.
  #
  # ── Why singleton-shaped ──
  #
  # There is one heartbeat per runtime ; there is one scheduler.
  # The scheduler's state is the tick counter (for observability)
  # plus the schedule registry. Per-rule state (when did THIS rule
  # last fire ?) lives on the Schedule entity, not on the rule
  # aggregate — keeping the macrophages clean of scheduling concern.
  #
  # ── What goes through the scheduler vs. what doesn't ──
  #
  # Goes through : any command that should fire on a tick cadence —
  # macrophage sweeps (Macrophage::*.Check with a target_list), chaos
  # actions (i617), future autonomic agents.
  #
  # Does NOT go through : reactive commands (Macrophage.Record*Edit
  # from the PostToolUse hook), explicit user commands, command-bus
  # routing in general. The scheduler is the cadence surface only ;
  # everything else stays in the existing dispatch path.

  aggregate "HeartbeatScheduler", "The rhythmic command dispatcher. Holds the tick counter and the Schedule entity collection ; each Tick advances the counter and dispatches every schedule whose cadence matches. Effectively a singleton — i611 phase 1 will convert this to a singleton-flagged aggregate." do
    identified_by :name

    attribute :name, Name

    # Session metadata
    attribute :session_started_at, Timestamp

    # The beat — incremented every Tick.
    attribute :current_tick, TickCount

    # Dispatch tallies — visible at a glance for observability.
    attribute :schedules_registered, RegistrationCount
    attribute :dispatches_fired,     DispatchCount

    # Last-operation surface for the statusline / quick reads.
    attribute :last_tick_at,     Timestamp
    attribute :last_dispatch_at, Timestamp
    attribute :last_dispatched,  ScheduledCommand

    # ── Value-objects : the language of rhythmic dispatch ──

    value_object "Name" do
      # Singleton key — there is exactly one HeartbeatScheduler per
      # runtime. Under i611 phase 1 (`singleton: true`), this VO
      # disappears.
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "Timestamp" do
      # ISO-8601 timestamp string. Used for session_started_at,
      # last_*_at fields, and per-dispatch recorded_at attributes.
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "TickCount" do
      # Monotonic count of heartbeats since session start. Resets
      # on StartSession ; persists across Tick dispatches inside the
      # session. The Schedule entity stores `last_dispatched_tick`
      # of this type so it can compute "next fire = last + cadence".
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    value_object "Cadence" do
      # Number of ticks between dispatches. A cadence of 100 means
      # the schedule fires every 100th tick. Cadence of 1 fires every
      # tick (chatty — only use for the cheapest checks). There is no
      # cadence-of-zero ; "I never want to fire on tick" means
      # "don't register a schedule".
      attribute :ticks, Integer
    end

    value_object "ScheduledCommand" do
      # The FQN of the command to dispatch when the schedule fires.
      # Form : "Domain::Aggregate.Command" (e.g.
      # "Macrophage::IdentityDiscipline.Check"). The scheduler
      # quotes this verbatim — does not validate that the command
      # exists at registration time ; runtime resolution catches a
      # typo when Dispatch fires.
      attribute :verb, String
    end

    value_object "TargetList" do
      # Comma-joined list of repo roots the dispatched command sweeps.
      # The receiving command (e.g. Macrophage::IdentityDiscipline.Check)
      # is responsible for parsing the list and walking each target.
      # Stored as a single string here so the bluebook stays a stub ;
      # once list-of-VO support firms up this becomes list_of(RepoPath).
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "ScheduleId" do
      # Stable slug identifying a registered schedule
      # (e.g. 'identity_discipline_sweep'). Used to look the schedule
      # up so the scheduler can record `last_dispatched_tick` on it.
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "RegistrationCount" do
      # Tally of how many RegisterSchedule commands have been
      # accepted in the current session.
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    value_object "DispatchCount" do
      # Tally of how many Dispatch commands have fired in the
      # current session. Across all schedules.
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    # ── CLI-cadence value-objects (i638 Unit E) ──
    #
    # The vocabulary of a CLI cadence invocation : `storehouse loop`
    # / `clock` / `run-loop`. The single kernel-floor
    # parse_cadence_invocation bridge (main.rs) parses argv into a
    # CadenceInvocation ; the run_loop / run_clock / run_pm_loop
    # shims then hold ONLY this typed value. The i630 FQN-truncation
    # bug class (`.split('.').last()` dropping the aggregate
    # qualifier) is structurally inexpressible here because no raw
    # FQN string survives the bridge — every command is a
    # ScheduledCommand. Bluebook = contract ; main.rs = the
    # kernel-floor mirror (consistent with 6ca50098 : the registry
    # belongs in main.rs as kernel-floor).

    value_object "CadenceInterval" do
      # Wall-clock interval between CLI-loop iterations, in
      # milliseconds. A documented TWIN of `Cadence` above, NOT a
      # merge : Cadence.ticks counts heartbeats for autonomic
      # dispatch (i616/i617 consumers — untouched) ; this is the
      # wall-clock sleep the CLI shims wait between iterations,
      # parsed once from "1s" / "500ms" / "2m" / "--poll <dur>".
      attribute :millis, Integer
    end

    value_object "GatePredicate" do
      # The `storehouse loop --gate <file.heki>:<field>=<value>`
      # predicate (i108). When the named heki field does not equal
      # `expected`, the shim sleeps WITHOUT dispatching. Typed so
      # the gate is a value, never a (String,String,String) tuple
      # sliced by hand in the handler.
      attribute :heki_path, String
      attribute :field,     String
      attribute :expected,  String
    end

    value_object "ClockSegment" do
      # One `storehouse clock --segment <lo>-<hi>:<FQN>` binding
      # (i107) : dispatch `command` while the local hour is within
      # [lo_hour, hi_hour] (wrap-around when lo_hour > hi_hour).
      # `command` is a ScheduledCommand so the FQN is never sliced.
      attribute :lo_hour, Integer
      attribute :hi_hour, Integer
      attribute :command, ScheduledCommand
    end

    value_object "CadenceInvocation" do
      # The parsed result of ONE CLI cadence invocation. The single
      # parse_cadence_invocation bridge produces this ; the shims
      # dispatch from `commands[idx].verb`. `commands` is the i106
      # rotation list (one FQN per tick) ; `interval` is the
      # wall-clock sleep ; `gate` is the optional i108 predicate ;
      # `segments` is the i107 clock-mode list. A single typed value
      # replacing the cmd_full / cmd_names / gate-tuple / segments
      # string surgery the three handlers do today.
      attribute :commands, list_of(ScheduledCommand)
      attribute :interval, CadenceInterval
      attribute :gate,     GatePredicate
      attribute :segments, list_of(ClockSegment)
    end

    # ── Entities — child records living inside the scheduler ──

    entity "Schedule" do
      description "One registered cadence binding. Says : every `cadence.ticks` ticks, dispatch `command` against `targets`. The scheduler walks every Schedule on each Tick and fires Dispatch for the ones whose `(current_tick - last_dispatched_tick) >= cadence.ticks`."
      attribute :id,                    ScheduleId
      attribute :command,               ScheduledCommand
      attribute :cadence,               Cadence
      attribute :targets,               TargetList
      attribute :last_dispatched_tick,  TickCount
      attribute :last_dispatched_at,    Timestamp
    end

    # ── Session lifecycle ──

    command "StartSession" do
      role "System"
      description "Reset the tick counter and dispatch tallies at session boot. The Schedule entity collection is NOT reset — schedules persist across sessions so a rule registers once and the scheduler honors it on every subsequent boot. Reset behavior is consistent with the macrophage : tallies are per-session, audit trail persists."
      attribute :session_started_at, Timestamp
      then_set :session_started_at, to: :session_started_at
      emits "SessionStarted"
    end

    # ── The pulse ──

    command "Tick" do
      role "System"
      description "One heartbeat. Advances current_tick by 1 and records last_tick_at. The runtime dispatches Tick on every statusline pulse ; the scheduler is responsible for then walking its Schedule entity collection and emitting Dispatch for any whose cadence matches (the dispatcher logic is in the runtime — the bluebook contract is just that Tick fires every pulse and Dispatch fires when cadence matches)."
      attribute :last_tick_at, Timestamp
      then_set :last_tick_at, to: :last_tick_at
      emits "Ticked"
    end

    # ── Schedule registration ──

    command "RegisterSchedule" do
      role "System"
      description "Declare a new cadence binding. id is a stable slug ; command is the FQN to dispatch ; cadence.ticks is how many heartbeats between dispatches ; targets is the comma-joined repo list the dispatched command should sweep. Registration is idempotent on id — registering the same id twice replaces the prior schedule with the new cadence / command / targets."
      attribute :id,      ScheduleId
      attribute :command, ScheduledCommand
      attribute :cadence, Cadence
      attribute :targets, TargetList
      emits "ScheduleRegistered"
    end

    command "RetireSchedule" do
      role "System"
      description "Remove a registered schedule. Once retired, the scheduler stops considering it on future Ticks. Used when a rule decides it no longer wants autonomic sweeps, or when reconfiguring at boot."
      attribute :id, ScheduleId
      emits "ScheduleRetired"
    end

    # ── The autonomic surface ──

    command "Dispatch" do
      role "System"
      description "Fire a registered schedule. Records last_dispatched_tick + last_dispatched_at on the matching Schedule entity, increments dispatches_fired, and updates the root's last_dispatched / last_dispatch_at surface. The runtime is responsible for ACTUALLY dispatching the command — this command records the intent and the audit trail ; the side effect (firing Macrophage::IdentityDiscipline.Check or similar) lands as a sidequest in the existing Cascade substrate."
      attribute :id,               ScheduleId
      attribute :dispatched_tick,  TickCount
      attribute :last_dispatch_at, Timestamp
      then_set :last_dispatch_at, to: :last_dispatch_at
      emits "Dispatched"
    end

    # ── CLI cadence surface (i638 Unit E) ──

    command "RunCadence" do
      role "System"
      description "Honor a CLI-parsed cadence invocation. The kernel-floor parse_cadence_invocation bridge parses `storehouse loop|clock|run-loop` argv into a CadenceInvocation ; this command records that the scheduler is running it and updates the last-dispatch surface. The run_loop / run_clock / run_pm_loop shims hold only the typed CadenceInvocation by the time they reach this surface — the i630 FQN-truncation bug class is structurally inexpressible because no raw FQN string survives the bridge."
      attribute :invocation,       CadenceInvocation
      attribute :last_dispatch_at, Timestamp
      then_set :last_dispatch_at, to: :last_dispatch_at
      emits "CadenceRan"
    end
  end

  # ── Consumer-contract — the runtime wiring ──
  #
  # The statusline renderer (rust/src/run_statusline/) dispatches
  # `HeartbeatScheduler.Tick` on every pulse via storehouse. The
  # runtime then walks the Schedule entity collection ; for each
  # schedule whose `(current_tick - last_dispatched_tick) >= cadence.ticks`
  # it dispatches `HeartbeatScheduler.Dispatch` for that schedule id
  # AND spawns the scheduled command as a sidequest. Both pieces of
  # wiring are out of scope for the i616 stub — the bluebook
  # establishes the contract ; companion cards wire it up.
end
